window.docContents[138].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--July___-2022":{"contents":"The second public release completes the first chapter.\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Introduction\u0009Release history","header":"July, 2022","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Introduction--Release-history--July___-2022"},"/#monad-transformer-order":{"contents":"When composing a monad from a stack of monad transformers, it's important to be aware that the order in which the monad transformers are layered matters.\nDifferent orderings of the same set of transformers result in different monads.\n\nThis version of  is just like the previous version, except it uses type classes to describe the set of available effects instead of providing a concrete monad:\n\n\n\nThe state and exception monad transformers can be combined in two different orders, each resulting in a monad that has instances of both type classes:\n\n\n\nWhen run on input for which the program does not throw an exception, both monads yield similar results:\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever, there is a subtle difference between these return values.\nIn the case of , the outermost constructor is , and it contains a pair of the unit constructor with the final state.\nIn the case of , the outermost constructor is the pair, which contains  applied only to the unit constructor.\nThe final state is outside of .\nIn both cases, the program returns the counts of vowels and consonants.\n\nOn the other hand, only one monad yields a count of vowels and consonants when the string causes an exception to be thrown.\nUsing , only an exception value is returned:\n\n\n\n\n\nUsing , the exception value is paired with the state as it was at the time that the exception was thrown:\n\n\n\n\n\nIt might be tempting to think that  is superior to  because it provides more information that might be useful when debugging.\nThe same program might compute different answers in  than it does in , and there's no principled reason to say that one of these answers is necessarily better than the other.\nThis can be seen by adding a step to the program that handles exceptions:\n\n\n\nThis program always succeeds, but it might succeed with different results.\nIf no exception is thrown, then the results are the same as :\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\nHowever, if the exception is thrown and caught, then the final states are very different.\nWith , the final state contains only the letter counts from :\n\n\n\n\n\nWith , the final state contains letter counts from both  and from , as one would expect in an imperative language:\n\n\n\n\n\nIn , throwing an exception “rolls back” the state to where the exception was caught.\nIn , modifications to the state persist across the throwing and catching of exceptions.\nThis difference can be seen by unfolding the definitions of  and .\n unfolds to , and  unfolds to .\nThat is to say,  describes functions that take an initial letter count, returning either an error or an  paired with updated counts.\nWhen an exception is thrown in , there is no final state.\n describes functions that take an initial letter count and return a new letter count paired with either an error or an .\nWhen an exception is thrown in , it is accompanied by a state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Monad Transformers","header":"6.3. Ordering Monad Transformers","id":"/#monad-transformer-order"}});